    <section class="section" id="introduction">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <span class="eyebrow">1 · Introduction</span>
          <h2>Why sentence types matter</h2>
          <p>Imagine writing this in your diary: “I woke up. I ran late. I forgot my bag. I reached school.” The message is clear, but it sounds flat. When you learn different sentence types, your writing becomes smoother, smarter, and more interesting.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="note-box">
            <h3>Real-life use</h3>
            <p>You use sentence types when telling a story, answering comprehension questions, writing emails, or explaining your ideas in class. Good writers mix sentence types to control pace and meaning.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="note-box">
            <h3>Exam advantage</h3>
            <p>In school assessments, sentence variety shows control. It helps you avoid repetition, improve grammar accuracy, and earn stronger writing marks.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="simple">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <span class="eyebrow">2A · Core concept</span>
          <h2>Simple sentences</h2>
          <p>A simple sentence has one subject, one main verb, and one complete thought. It contains one independent clause, which means it can stand alone and make sense by itself.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="card">
            <span class="pill">Structure</span>
            <p><strong>Subject + Verb + Complete thought</strong></p>
            <div class="example"><span class="label-subject">[Birds]</span> <span class="label-predicate">{fly.}</span></div>
            <div class="example"><span class="label-subject">[She]</span> <span class="label-predicate">{reads every night.}</span></div>
            <div class="example"><span class="label-subject">[The old cat]</span> <span class="label-predicate">{slept on the sofa.}</span></div>
            <div class="example"><span class="label-subject">[My brother and I]</span> <span class="label-predicate">{laughed loudly.}</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <span class="pill">Key idea</span>
            <p>A simple sentence can be short or long. It is called simple because it has only one main clause, not because it has few words.</p>
            <ul class="mistake-list">
              <li>Fragment: “Ran to the bus stop.” → missing subject.</li>
              <li>Incomplete thought: “The children in the park.” → missing verb.</li>
              <li>Confusion: a long sentence with one clause is still simple.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="card-grid" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
          <div class="card"><h3>More examples</h3><p>The sun shone brightly.</p><p>Our class visited the museum.</p><p>The tiny puppy barked at the gate.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>Medium examples</h3><p>The excited crowd cheered for the team.</p><p>My neighbour grows roses in her garden.</p><p>The train arrived exactly on time.</p></div>
          <div class="card"><h3>Spot the subject</h3><p>In “The train arrived exactly on time,” the subject is <em>the train</em> and the predicate is <em>arrived exactly on time</em>.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="compound">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <span class="eyebrow">2B · Core concept</span>
          <h2>Compound sentences</h2>
          <p>A compound sentence joins two independent clauses. Each side can stand alone, but they are linked because the ideas are connected.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="card">
            <span class="pill">FANBOYS</span>
            <p>Use coordinating conjunctions: <strong>for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so</strong>.</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>And</strong> adds information.</li>
              <li><strong>But</strong> shows contrast.</li>
              <li><strong>So</strong> shows result.</li>
              <li><strong>Or</strong> gives a choice.</li>
              <li><strong>Yet</strong> shows surprising contrast.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <span class="pill">Structure</span>
            <p><strong>Independent clause + comma + FANBOYS + independent clause</strong></p>
            <div class="example">I was hungry, <strong>so</strong> I made a sandwich.</div>
            <div class="example">Maya wanted to swim, <strong>but</strong> the pool was closed.</div>
            <div class="example">We can walk home, <strong>or</strong> we can wait for the bus.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="card-grid" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Compare with simple</h3>
            <p><strong>Simple:</strong> The rain stopped.</p>
            <p><strong>Compound:</strong> The rain stopped, and the children ran outside.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Common errors</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Missing comma: “I was tired but I kept going.”</li>
              <li>Run-on: “I was tired I kept going.”</li>
              <li>Too many joined clauses in one sentence.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Extra examples</h3>
            <p>The bell rang, and the class went quiet.</p>
            <p>He trained hard, yet he felt nervous.</p>
            <p>Sara called me, so I answered at once.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="complex">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <span class="eyebrow">2C · Core concept</span>
          <h2>Complex sentences</h2>
          <p>A complex sentence has one independent clause and at least one dependent clause. The dependent clause cannot stand alone, so it needs the main clause to complete the meaning.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="card">
            <span class="pill">Independent vs dependent</span>
            <p><strong>Independent clause:</strong> She went to bed.</p>
            <p><strong>Dependent clause:</strong> after she finished her homework</p>
            <div class="example">She went to bed after she finished her homework.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <span class="pill">Subordinating conjunctions</span>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Time:</strong> when, while, after, before, until</li>
              <li><strong>Reason:</strong> because, since, as</li>
              <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> although, though, even though</li>
              <li><strong>Condition:</strong> if, unless</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="card-grid" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Examples</h3>
            <p>Because it was raining, we stayed indoors.</p>
            <p>Although she was tired, she finished the project.</p>
            <p>When the bell rang, the students rushed out.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Clause placement</h3>
            <p><strong>Because it was cold,</strong> I wore a jacket.</p>
            <p>I wore a jacket <strong>because it was cold.</strong></p>
            <p>The meaning stays similar, but the emphasis changes.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Common errors</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Using a dependent clause as a full sentence.</li>
              <li>Forgetting the comma when the dependent clause comes first.</li>
              <li>Confusing “because” sentences with compound sentences.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="comparison">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <span class="eyebrow">3 · Comparison</span>
          <h2>See the differences quickly</h2>
          <p>Use this table when revising. It helps you compare structure, clauses, examples, and clue words in one place.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Sentence type</th>
                <th>Structure</th>
                <th>Number of clauses</th>
                <th>Example</th>
                <th>Key identifiers</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Simple</strong></td>
                <td>One independent clause</td>
                <td>1</td>
                <td>The cat slept on the rug.</td>
                <td>One complete thought, no joining word needed</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Compound</strong></td>
                <td>Independent clause + comma + FANBOYS + independent clause</td>
                <td>2 or more independent clauses</td>
                <td>The cat slept on the rug, but the dog stayed awake.</td>
                <td>FANBOYS, two complete ideas</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Complex</strong></td>
                <td>Independent clause + dependent clause</td>
                <td>1 independent + 1 or more dependent</td>
                <td>Although the cat slept on the rug, the dog stayed awake.</td>
                <td>Because, although, when, if, while, unless</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="visuals">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <span class="eyebrow">4 · Visual learning</span>
          <h2>Structure diagrams and learning links</h2>
          <p>Visual patterns help you remember grammar faster. Use these diagrams to picture how each sentence is built.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="diagram">SIMPLE SENTENCE
┌────────────┐   ┌──────────────────────┐
│ SUBJECT    │ → │ PREDICATE            │
└────────────┘   └──────────────────────┘
The bird        sang at sunrise.

One complete thought.</div>
          <div class="diagram">COMPOUND SENTENCE
[Independent clause] , FANBOYS [Independent clause]
The bird sang, and the cat listened.

Both sides can stand alone.</div>
          <div class="diagram">COMPLEX SENTENCE
Because it was raining, we stayed inside.
└ dependent clause ┘  └ independent clause ┘

If the dependent clause comes first,
use a comma.</div>
          <div class="diagram">CLAUSE SPOTTER
1. Find the verb.
2. Find the subject.
3. Ask: can this part stand alone?
4. If yes, it is independent.
5. If no, it is dependent.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="resource-list" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
          <div class="resource-item"><strong>BBC Bitesize</strong><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/articles/zbvqkty" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Simple, compound and complex sentences</a><p>Clear explanations, quizzes, and a student-friendly format.</p></div>
          <div class="resource-item"><strong>Twinkl</strong><a href="https://www.twinkl.com/blog/help-with-simple-compound-and-complex-sentences" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Help with simple, compound and complex sentences</a><p>Examples and child-friendly breakdowns for practice.</p></div>
          <div class="resource-item"><strong>K5 Learning</strong><a href="https://www.k5learning.com/blog/simple-compound-complex-sentences" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Simple, compound and complex sentences</a><p>Free worksheet support and extra examples.</p></div>
          <div class="resource-item"><strong>YouTube</strong><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Uxtfb5GCLrc" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Four English Sentence Types</a><p>Short visual explanation for revision.</p></div>
          <div class="resource-item"><strong>Education.com</strong><a href="https://www.education.com/resources/worksheets/english-language-arts/grammar-mechanics/simple-compound-complex-sentences/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Printable sentence worksheets</a><p>Extra printable activities for home practice.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="practice">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <span class="eyebrow">5 · Guided practice</span>
          <h2>10 practice questions</h2>
          <p>Try these questions on your own first. Then check the answers below.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="practice-list">
          <div class="practice-item">1. The wind blew across the field. → Identify the sentence type.</div>
          <div class="practice-item">2. I studied hard, so I passed the test. → Identify the sentence type.</div>
          <div class="practice-item">3. Although it was late, she kept reading. → Identify the sentence type.</div>
          <div class="practice-item">4. The dog barked, and the baby cried. → Identify the sentence type.</div>
          <div class="practice-item">5. My cousins played football in the park. → Identify the sentence type.</div>
          <div class="practice-item">6. Convert to a compound sentence: I was tired. I finished my homework.</div>
          <div class="practice-item">7. Convert to a complex sentence: She was hungry. She waited for dinner.</div>
          <div class="practice-item">8. Convert to a complex sentence: It rained heavily. We stayed inside.</div>
          <div class="practice-item">9. Fill in the blank: I wanted to go outside, ______ the storm had started.</div>
          <div class="practice-item">10. Fill in the blank: ______ you practise every day, you will improve.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="answers">
          <h3>Suggested answers</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>1. Simple</li>
            <li>2. Compound</li>
            <li>3. Complex</li>
            <li>4. Compound</li>
            <li>5. Simple</li>
            <li>6. I was tired, but I finished my homework.</li>
            <li>7. Although she was hungry, she waited for dinner.</li>
            <li>8. Because it rained heavily, we stayed inside.</li>
            <li>9. but / because, depending on how you rewrite the sentence</li>
            <li>10. If</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="challenge">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <span class="eyebrow">6 · Challenge</span>
          <h2>Higher-level thinking</h2>
          <p>These tasks test your correction skills, grammar knowledge, and ability to transform sentences carefully.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card-grid">
          <div class="challenge-item"><h3>1. Correct the error</h3><p>She was nervous but she smiled and she answered every question.</p></div>
          <div class="challenge-item"><h3>2. Rewrite</h3><p>Change this into a complex sentence using <em>although</em>: The weather was terrible. The match continued.</p></div>
          <div class="challenge-item"><h3>3. Identify clauses</h3><p>Unless the team practises regularly, they will not improve.</p></div>
          <div class="challenge-item"><h3>4. Add punctuation</h3><p>Because the lights went out the children screamed.</p></div>
          <div class="challenge-item"><h3>5. Create your own</h3><p>Write one simple, one compound, and one complex sentence about your favourite hobby.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="application">
      <div class="container writing-task">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">7 · Real-life application</span>
          <h2>Paragraph writing task</h2>
          <p>Write a short paragraph about a memorable day. It could be Sports Day, a birthday, a school trip, or even a rainy afternoon at home.</p>
          <ul class="tip-list" style="margin-top: var(--space-4);">
            <li>Use at least 2 simple sentences.</li>
            <li>Use at least 2 compound sentences.</li>
            <li>Use at least 2 complex sentences.</li>
            <li>Label each sentence S, C, or CX after writing.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="note-box">
          <h3>Model paragraph</h3>
          <p>It was Sports Day at school. <strong>(S)</strong> The field looked bright, and the whole class felt excited. <strong>(C)</strong> Although I was nervous, I joined the running race. <strong>(CX)</strong> The whistle blew, and we ran as fast as we could. <strong>(C)</strong> I crossed the line first. <strong>(S)</strong> Because I had practised for weeks, I felt proud of myself. <strong>(CX)</strong></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="revision">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-intro">
          <span class="eyebrow">8 &amp; 9 · Revision and memory tricks</span>
          <h2>Quick revision notes</h2>
          <p>Use these points for fast review before a test or quiz.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card-grid">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Simple</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>One independent clause</li>
              <li>One complete thought</li>
              <li>Can be short or long</li>
              <li>Example: The baby slept peacefully.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Compound</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Two independent clauses</li>
              <li>Joined by FANBOYS</li>
              <li>Usually needs a comma before the conjunction</li>
              <li>Example: I wanted to play, but I had homework.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Complex</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>One independent clause + one dependent clause</li>
              <li>Uses words like because, although, when, if</li>
              <li>Comma after the dependent clause if it comes first</li>
              <li>Example: Because it was late, we went home.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
          <div class="note-box">
            <h3>Memory tricks</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>FANBOYS</strong> = For, And, Nor, But, Or, Yet, So</li>
              <li><strong>Stand-alone test:</strong> can this clause make sense by itself?</li>
              <li><strong>Complex clue:</strong> words like because, although, when, while, if, unless</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="note-box">
            <h3>Sentence spotting in exams</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>If both halves can stand alone and a FANBOYS word joins them, it is probably compound.</li>
              <li>If one part cannot stand alone, it is probably complex.</li>
              <li>If there is only one main clause, it is simple.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

